  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on travel time estimates from a proprietary API rather than observed real-world emergency transport times.",
      "what": "This study evaluated geographic accessibility to comprehensive emergency obstetric and newborn care (EmONC) across all 29 districts of the Greater Accra Region using travel time estimates from the Google Maps Directions API. It calculated median travel times and the proportion of reproductive-age women within 15-, 30-, and 60-minute thresholds of both public and private facilities during peak and non-peak traffic. The median travel time to the nearest public EmONC facility was 29.7 minutes during peak traffic, with 90% of women living within a 30-minute travel window.",
      "why": "The analysis demonstrates how traffic congestion and peri-urban location compound socioeconomic disparities, revealing that peak-hour traffic completely cuts off peri-urban women from reaching any public emergency facility within the critical 30-minute window while wealthier urban populations maintain superior access."
    }
  },
